UberSDR: publish when each channel's samples were captured

WHAT
  Estimate, in the RX888 USB callback, the CLOCK_REALTIME at which input sample
  0 was taken, and publish per channel a pair in its status packets:

      CAPTURE_TS_REF     (240)  RTP timestamp of the first frame of the latest block
      CAPTURE_TIME_REF   (241)  CLOCK_REALTIME ns at which that frame's signal was captured
      CAPTURE_GENERATION (242)  bumped whenever the anchor is re-established

  With one pair and the stream's RTP clock rate, a receiver can give every
  packet its capture time:  capture = TIME_REF + (int32)(ts - TS_REF) / rate.

HOW
  The A/D clock is GPSDO-locked, so t_capture(n) = t0 + n / samprate and only t0
  needs estimating.  Every transfer gives one observation, callback time minus
  the time its last sample represents, which can only exceed t0.  capture_note()
  keeps the floor of those over a 10 s window of per-second minima (the floor,
  not the mean: the delay is one-sided with a long scheduling tail).  It starts
  over when a transfer fails; re-anchors when 50 transfers in a row, spread over
  at least 150 ms, all sit >2 ms above the anchor (an unflagged loss or a forward
  clock step -- the span rules out a backlog completing back to back), or when a
  whole second does; and follows a lower floor at once (a backward clock step).
  Each re-anchoring bumps the generation.  The anchor is stored before the
  generation is bumped and read after it, so a channel that sees a new
  generation always sees the anchor that goes with it.

  capture_mark(), at the end of downconvert(), pairs the RTP timestamp the
  block's first frame will carry (rtp.timestamp plus frames still queued from
  earlier blocks) with t0 + sample_index / samprate, less the linear-phase FIR's
  group delay of (M-1)/2 input samples, and filter2's if it is in use.

  Keeping the pair honest:
  - It is dropped when a demod restarts, which re-presets the RTP timestamps.
  - It records the RTP rate it was made at and is withheld from status while the
    channel's rate differs: a command that changes the rate is answered before
    the demod restarts, and the old pair must not go out labelled with the new
    rate.
  - A block that execute_filter_output() hands back as zeros, without advancing
    sample_index (the channel fell a whole ring behind), does not re-pair; the
    existing pair still holds, since those zeros take up a block of timestamps.
  - A receiver keeps using the last pair it was sent, so the channel sends
    status on the metadata group within a few blocks (staggered by SSRC)
    whenever that pair changes meaning: when a new one is made after a restart
    or a re-anchoring, and when the old one stops being valid -- the anchor is
    being rebuilt after a failed transfer, the demod restarted, or the channel
    went idle outside the front end's coverage (its timestamps stop advancing).
    Status then carries no pair, which a receiver should take as "none".

  Times are CLOCK_REALTIME, not GPS_TIME: it is the clock the kernel stamps
  received datagrams with (SO_TIMESTAMPNS), so a receiver on the same host
  subtracts the two directly and no leap-second table is involved.

  Precision relative to the host clock: the floor settles within a few us in
  simulation, recovers within ~2 us a second after a flagged loss and within
  ~200 ms of an unflagged one, and rides out a 300 ms event-thread stall
  without moving.  The floor still contains a constant hardware latency (FX3
  buffering, the last USB packet in flight, A/D pipeline), estimated at 50-300 us.
  It belongs to the front end, so rx888.c takes RX888_CAPTURE_LATENCY_NS off every
  arrival time before capture_note() sees it.  It is 0 until measured: ubersdr-ntp's
  class delta (radio consensus minus a GPS-fed stratum 1 server) measures it.

WHY CONFIG OR UBERSDR CANNOT DO THIS
  Only radiod sees the moment samples arrive and knows which input sample each
  RTP timestamp came from.  UberSDR alone can see packet arrival, which includes
  the 20 ms block fill and all of radiod's processing.

TAGS
  Numbered 240-242 in their own enum, so upstream appending to enum status_type
  cannot renumber or collide with them.  Receivers that don't know them skip
  them, as ka9q's decode_status.c and UberSDR already do for unknown tags.

DROP IT WHEN
  Upstream grows an equivalent capture-time reference.  Worth offering them.

